course: dictionary
lesson: 10938
title: Describing how we move
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Waddle means to walk with short steps, swaying from side to side, like a duck or a penguin. Look for the clue in the sentence: «On land penguins waddle like clumsy toys» tells you the walk looks clumsy, not fast.
explain: Read the context to check your meaning. «Penguins waddle because their legs are short and set far back» gives the reason: legs that are short and set far back make the body sway. Try the swap test: put "walk with short swaying steps" in place of waddle and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Waddle can also be a noun, meaning the swaying walk itself: «The toddler walked with a funny waddle.» When you describe a person's walk, be kind. It can sound like teasing, so describe the walk, not the person.
fact: Waddle means to walk with short steps, swaying from side to side.
fact: Waddle can be a verb (he waddles, she waddled) or a noun (a waddle).
fact: Ducks, geese and penguins waddle, and their legs are short and set far back.
fact: Penguins waddle on land, but in the water they swim very fast.
fact: Penguins sometimes slide on their bellies instead, because sliding is faster.
exercise: «On land penguins waddle like clumsy toys.» What does waddle mean? | Jump high into the air | *Walk with short steps, swaying from side to side | Sit still and keep warm | Swim very fast through the water
exercise: «The toddler walked with a funny waddle.» In this sentence, what is waddle? | The name of a place | A verb: to dive deep under water | A kind of bird | *A noun: a swaying walk
exercise: «Penguins waddle because their legs are short and set far back.» Why do penguins waddle? | They are cold and want to hurry | They like to dance | *Their legs are short and set far back | Their wings are too big to fly
test: «They row Portly back, set him down on the tow-path near the ford, and watch him waddle off until Otter leaps up.» What does waddle off mean here? | *Walk away with short swaying steps | Fall fast asleep | Hurry off at full speed | Jump into the river
test: Oliver says, "Grandma waddles when she walks." Why should a kind reader be careful with waddle about a person? | It is always a compliment about speed | It only ever describes birds | *It can sound like teasing, so describe the walk kindly | It means she is happy and fast
exam: Which sentence uses waddle as a noun, meaning a swaying walk? | «The duck can waddle down to the water.» | *«The toddler walked with a funny waddle.» | «The gentoo penguin swims very fast.» | «Penguins waddle along the ice.»
